"Greg Sumner" <gsumner@nwlinkNospam.com> wrote in message news:<g42dnYhjaYpN4-HdRVn-jw@comcast.com>...
> So, what is your strategy?

I don't know why people find Monks hard; I suspect it's just being too
used to weapon and armor users.  My first ascension was a Monk, and
before that I was getting them down to Medusa fairly regularly.

My strategy wasn't particularly special.  No body armor, no melee
weapons (carry some ranged weapons [daggers/darts] for emergencies,
but you probably won't need them).  Stick with your robe, spells are
good.  Healing is the most useful to start with, but sleep is not to
be scoffed at (it can save you from tough quadrupeds and insects). 
You can also go places with offensive spells, though you won't be able
to use them against everything.  You don't need a pet, and since
you're fast waiting around for it gets dull.

I recommend going first to Minetown so you're sure to have an altar
and you'll pick up a helm and shoes.  You should aim for level 6 or 7
in Minetown.  Also get a pickaxe if possible.  If you find a magic
lamp here or otherwise get an early wish, I like gauntlets of power
(speed boots are another possibility).  Don't go lower than Minetown
yet - go do Sokoban (using the pickaxe in case something goes wrong -
giant mimics etc).  Now go down for the luckstone in Mine's End, which
should be pretty easy.

Once you have that, it's down to the Castle.  Wish for what you need
out of gauntlets of power, speed boots, amulet of reflection, bag of
holding, magic marker.  Once you feel *extremely* prepared, you can
take on Master Kaen.  Don't underestimate him.  I recommend levitating
over the lava for a while to get a feel for how he acts and to dispose
of the other monsters, then take him out however you can (keep in mind
that projectiles will make you drift backwards).

Once he's dead the rest of the game is pretty easy, even unarmed and
unarmored.  Remember to use some spells (healing, identify,
fireball/cone of cold are the most important); you have plenty of
slots to improve your skill in them after you reach grandmaster
martial arts.  It will probably take a few more hits than you're used
to to kill monsters in Gehennom, but it's still easy.

If you're having trouble, breaking vegetarian conduct is not the end
of the world.  You feel a bit guilty, but that's worth it for wraiths
and giants (and black dragons).

